Why the AGPL, specifically
--------------------------

Kinfold's entire value is that you can verify the operator cannot read your
family's data. A permissive licence would let someone run a modified, hosted
copy that quietly logs plaintext, weakens the crypto, or exfiltrates keys,
with no obligation to tell anyone.

Section 13 of the AGPL closes that gap: anyone who runs a modified version as
a network service must offer its users the complete corresponding source of
that modified version. Combined with the architecture — records are encrypted
in the browser, the server holds only ciphertext and wrapped keys — this is
what makes "we cannot read your tree" a checkable claim rather than a promise.


A request, not a licence term
-----------------------------

The people described in a family tree did not sign up and cannot object. If
you run an instance, please keep that data inside the application's own
purpose: do not sell it, feed it to analytics or advertising, train models on
it, or hand it to a third party, and honour a request to be removed.

This paragraph is a statement of intent. It adds no condition to the AGPL and
is not a "further restriction" under section 7 — the licence terms are exactly
those in the LICENSE file, nothing more.
